### What is the problem?

Given an array, reverse the order of its elements in place. In a banking context, Rajesh's transaction history is stored oldest first at National Bank of India, but the mobile app needs to display newest first.

### How does the two-pointer swap work?

Set `left` to index 0 and `right` to the last index. Swap `array[left]` and `array[right]`, then increment `left` and decrement `right`. Continue while `left < right`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class StatementReverser {

    public static void reverseInPlace(BigDecimal[] transactions) {
        if (transactions == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Transactions cannot be null");
        }
        int left = 0;
        int right = transactions.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            BigDecimal temp = transactions[left];
            transactions[left] = transactions[right];
            transactions[right] = temp;
            left++;
            right--;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] txns = {
            new BigDecimal("1500.00"),
            new BigDecimal("8200.00"),
            new BigDecimal("3400.00"),
            new BigDecimal("12000.00"),
            new BigDecimal("500.00")
        };
        System.out.println("Before: " + Arrays.toString(txns));
        reverseInPlace(txns);
        System.out.println("After:  " + Arrays.toString(txns));
    }
}
```

```text
Before: [1500.00, 8200.00, 3400.00, 12000.00, 500.00]
After:  [500.00, 12000.00, 3400.00, 8200.00, 1500.00]
```

### Why does the middle element stay in place for odd-length arrays?

When the array has an odd number of elements, the two pointers eventually land on the same middle element. The condition `left < right` stops the loop because `left` is no longer strictly less than `right`. The middle element is already in its correct position because the reversal mirrors around it.

### Why is in-place reversal preferred?

The naive approach creates a new array and copies elements backwards, which uses O(n) extra space. The in-place approach uses only a single temporary variable for the swap, so it is O(1) extra space. For a mobile app displaying a long statement, the extra memory matters.

### What is the time and space complexity?

- **Time: O(n)** because the loop runs n/2 times, each doing a constant-time swap.
- **Space: O(1)** because only one temporary variable is used.

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using `left <= right` instead of `left < right`. With `<=`, when the array has an odd length, the middle element would be swapped with itself, which is harmless but wasteful. More dangerously, if the loop continued past the middle, it would swap elements back to their original positions, undoing the reversal.

```java
// Mistake: using <= allows unnecessary self-swap
while (left <= right) { /* middle element swaps with itself */ }

// Correct: strict less-than stops at the middle
while (left < right) { /* clean stop */ }
```

### How is this different from creating a reversed copy?

In-place reversal modifies the caller's array, which is a side effect. A reversed copy creates a new array and leaves the original unchanged. In production I offer both methods and document the difference.

```java
// Reversed copy (does not modify original)
public static BigDecimal[] reversedCopy(BigDecimal[] original) {
    BigDecimal[] result = new BigDecimal[original.length];
    for (int i = 0; i < original.length; i++) {
        result[i] = original[original.length - 1 - i];
    }
    return result;
}
```

### How does array reversal relate to rotation?

Rotating an array by k positions can be done with three reversals: reverse the whole array, reverse the first k elements, and reverse the remaining elements. This is the reversal-based rotation algorithm, which runs in O(n) time and O(1) space.

